# Tests must not touch machine state (R1)

This workspace ships long-running services, elevated configurators,
operator GUIs, and headless installers that all read from the same
machine-global stores: `%PROGRAMDATA%\AIC\…` / `/etc/aic/…`, Windows
`HKLM`, Windows Credential Manager / macOS Keychain / Linux Secret
Service, named pipes under `\\.\pipe\…`, and process environment
variables. A test that mutates any of those is a **test-isolation bug**:
parallel tests in the same process see each other's mutations, the
developer's real installation gets corrupted, and the test passes on
Tuesday and fails on Wednesday because a sibling ran first.

## The rule

> **Tests must drive the system under test through explicit per-test
> paths, handles, and channels — never through `std::env::set_var`, never
> through writes to shared machine stores. Production code that reads
> from machine-global state must expose a fixture API
> (`fn save_to_file_at(&Path)`, `fn open_at(channel: &str)`,
> `fn load_from_dir(&Path)`) so tests can bind their own sandbox without
> mutating the process.**

1. **No `std::env::set_var(` in test bodies** — `#[test]`,
   `#[tokio::test]`, `#[test_log::test]`, `rstest::*`, criterion
   benchmarks, `proptest!` blocks. The process environment is a single
   global; concurrent tests racing on it is undefined behavior in Rust
   1.74+ (`set_var` is `unsafe` for exactly this reason).
2. **Production code that reads an env var to locate a directory / pipe /
   file MUST also expose an explicit-path variant** taking the resolved
   path or handle as an argument. Tests bind their own
   `tempfile::TempDir` and call that variant; the env-var path remains as
   the production operator-controlled debug knob, but the test path does
   not traverse it.
3. **No test may write to a shared machine store** unless it lives under
   a per-test sandbox the test created and deletes on drop:
   - `%PROGRAMDATA%\AIC\…` / `/etc/aic/…` → a `tempfile::TempDir` passed
     explicitly via the fixture API.
   - Windows `HKLM` / `HKCU` → the `pim-config-storage` in-memory test
     backend (`cfg(test)`), or a throwaway
     `HKCU\Software\AIC\test-<uuid>` subtree deleted in `Drop`.
   - OS keyring → the in-memory backend with a **throwaway program id**
     (`pim-e2e-<uuid>`); never a production program id.
   - Named pipes / Unix sockets → a per-test name with a UUID suffix
     (`\\.\pipe\aic-test-{uuid}-elevation`) passed explicitly to the
     server / client constructors.

## Existing fixtures — use these, do not invent new ones

| Need | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Throwaway program id for keyring / HKLM tests | `pim_offline_client::secret_storage::elevated_local_storage_tests::test_program_id()` — unique `pim-e2e-<uuid>` per test |
| Sandboxed config + log dir | `tempfile::TempDir::new()?` plus the explicit-path variant on the type under test |
| In-memory `pim-config-storage` backend | `pim_config_storage::InMemoryBackend::new()` under `cfg(test)` |
| Sandboxed pipe / socket name | `format!("{}aic-test-{}-elev", PIPE_PREFIX, uuid::Uuid::new_v4())` then `IpcServer::bind_at` / `IpcClient::connect_at` |
| Mock SCM status sink | `pim_shutdown::scm::MockStatusSink` (see `pim-shutdown/docs/INTEGRATION.md`) |

## Forbidden constructs

In every file under `tests/`, `**/*_tests.rs`, `**/tests.rs`,
`**/integration_tests.rs`, `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { … }` blocks, and any
`examples/` that asserts behavior:

- `std::env::set_var(…)` / `env::set_var(…)` — banned outright.
- `std::env::remove_var(…)` — same reason.
- `std::fs::create_dir_all("/etc/aic/…")` / `"C:\\ProgramData\\AIC\\…"`.
- `winreg::enums::HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` opens — use HKCU under a
  `test-<uuid>` subkey or the in-memory backend.
- `keyring::Entry::new("pim-offline-agent", …)` / any production
  service-name string — use `test_program_id()`.
- Hard-coded pipe paths
  (`r"\\.\pipe\aic-offline-agent-service\elevation"`) — bind a unique
  per-test name.
- `TcpListener::bind("0.0.0.0:8443")` / any deterministic port — use
  `bind("127.0.0.1:0")` and read the port back via `local_addr()`.

## Required production-code pattern

Every type reading a path / channel / handle from a machine-global store
exposes two surfaces — a production resolver and an explicit fixture
variant:

```rust
impl Settings {
    /// Production: resolves OFFLINE_DEBUG_CONFIG_DIR if set, else the
    /// platform default under %PROGRAMDATA% / /etc.
    pub fn save_to_default_location(&self) -> Result<()> {
        let dir = Self::default_config_dir()?;
        self.save_to_file_at(&dir.join("settings.json"))
    }

    /// Fixture: explicit, no env-var read, no platform lookup.
    pub fn save_to_file_at(&self, path: &Path) -> Result<()> {
        let bytes = serde_json::to_vec_pretty(self).context("serialize Settings")?;
        std::fs::write(path, bytes).with_context(|| format!("write {}", path.display()))?;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation** (`pim-offline-client/tests/integration_tests.rs:125`):

```rust
let temp_dir = tempfile::TempDir::new()?;
std::env::set_var("OFFLINE_DEBUG_CONFIG_DIR", temp_dir.path());
let settings = Settings::load_from_default_location()?;
```

**Fix** — bind the sandbox explicitly; the env-var production path stays
intact for operators debugging, and tests stop racing on it:

```rust
let temp_dir = tempfile::TempDir::new()?;
let settings = Settings::load_from_dir(temp_dir.path())?;
```

Any **new** env-var read must arrive with its matching fixture API in the
same change set, and the fixture-replacement inventory in
`core-assets/docs/dev/rule-enforcement-status.md` must be updated. That
document also carries the per-repo adoption status. A PR that grows a new
`env::set_var` in a test body is rejected at review.

